**Explanation of the change:**

1. **`one_pass` is removed entirely** (the plan says to replace the first-improvement sweep machinery) and its logic is folded into a single `local_search`, so the call site `best_seq, best_cost = local_search(...)` and ILS usage remain unchanged — the new mechanism executes on the normal return path.

2. **Don't-look bits:** each position starts unmarked (don't-look=False). When no improving move is found at position `i`, its bit is set and it is skipped thereafter. When a move is accepted, bits for every position in `[lo-1, hi+1]` are cleared so only genuinely affected regions are re-examined. This converts repeated O(n²) full sweeps into a mostly-O(n) incremental evaluation pattern.

3. **Best-improvement within the neighborhood:** at each active position we pick the best or-opt reinsertion or adjacent swap (first move found is no longer accepted blindly), giving a steeper VND descent per oracle call.

4. **Compute budget unchanged:** same 85s deadline, same cache, same beam/ILS skeleton, no new optimizer starts. All candidates are full permutations scored by `workload.get_opt_seq_cost`, so validity and the identity-permutation fallback are preserved. The expected gain is a strictly larger number of distinct local optima explored per second by the ruin-and-recreate ILS loop.
